  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nWith the moisture plume just to the west of the Panhandles as we \nhead into Saturday, it will hold pretty steady along and just east \nof the state line providing a medium chance 30-50% of showers and \nmaybe an isolated storm to the Adrian, Dalhart, Boise City line. \nEast of that line the chances drop off pretty sharply so much that \nAmarillo is less than 10 percent.  Now for areas that will see \nrain/storms, it could get several rounds and the possibility of \nstorms to produce ample rain amount that could lead to some \nlocalized flooding conditions.  Right now the confidence is very low \nfor Saturday.  As we head into Sunday that southerly flow starts to \nbreak down into a more southwesterly flow and moisture starts to \nmove across the Panhandles so much that the central Panhandles will \nhave a high chance >70 percent of showers/storms and there may be a \nlittle bit of a convective component Sunday afternoon to these rain \nshowers that could enhance rain amounts.  Overall, NBM model still \nholds about a medium to high chance of 1\" of rain for the western \nand central Panhandles for Sunday.  As we move into Monday and \nTuesday the moisture will continue to track further east, and \neventually more south, this is where the eastern Panhandles will \nhave a better shot a rain chances.  Although there is some question \nas to how much residual moisture will linger to the eastern \nPanhandles, the probabilities are fairly high (60-80%) for at least a \nquarter of an inch from Sunday night through Tuesday morning. Monday \nnight into Tuesday will have the cold front move through the \nPanhandles and the precipitation chances will drop off.  The front \nwill be fairly mild as highs on Tuesday are still expected to be in \nthe 60s across most of the area, so not too strong.  Wednesday we'll \nreturn to more southwesterly flow and warmer air will be in place, \nwith highs returning to the mid to upper 60s, maybe even low 70s in \nthe warm spots. \n\nWeber",
